# compdump
#
# zsh writes the completion system's tables to a dump file that the next
# compinit reads back instead of scanning $fpath.  This shell never writes
# that file: it would be read by the other shell too, and the two must not
# trade one another's files.  compinit keeps a dump of its own instead, of
# what its scan found, under ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/sh/ (#6307) — which
# is not this function's to rewrite, since a table a startup file added after
# compinit is not something a scan found.  So this is the name a startup file
# may call, and it does nothing, at 0.  See compinit, and
# docs/spec/functions.md.

return 0
